---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE_LAUNCH_DATE } from '../lib/seo';
---
<Base
  title="About TrafficChallan"
  description="TrafficChallan is an independent, non-government reference on Indian traffic e-challans: fine amounts, portals, and how to check or pay."
  path="/about/"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Home', path: '/' }, { name: 'About', path: '/about/' }]}
  dateModified={SITE_LAUNCH_DATE}
>
  <h1>About TrafficChallan</h1>

  <p>
    TrafficChallan is an independent reference site for Indian traffic e-challans. We explain what a
    fine actually costs under the Motor Vehicles Act, how the amount changes from state to state, and
    which official portal to use to check a challan or pay it. We built the site because the rules are
    scattered across parivahan.gov.in, a dozen state transport and police portals, and periodic gazette
    notifications — and most people just want a straight answer to "what will this cost me" or "where do
    I pay this."
  </p>

  <h2>What we are not</h2>
  <p>
    We are not a government website, and we are not run by or affiliated with the Ministry of Road
    Transport and Highways, any state police department, or any transport authority. We do not check the
    status of your challan against a live government database, and we never collect a payment from you.
    When it's time to actually check or pay a challan, every page on this site points you to the correct
    official portal — that's where the transaction happens, not here.
  </p>

  <h2>How we keep the facts straight</h2>
  <p>
    Every fine amount, section reference, and procedural step on this site is sourced to an official
    document — a government portal, a police department notice, or a gazette notification — and that
    source is linked on the page it appears on. Each page also carries a "last verified" date. We re-check
    every page against its sources on a rolling 90-day cycle, and our build process is set up to flag any
    page whose facts haven't been re-verified inside that window, so stale information doesn't sit
    published indefinitely. Fines change when states issue new notifications; when we find a change, we
    update the page and the date changes with it.
  </p>

  <h2>Who runs this</h2>
  <p>
    TrafficChallan is built and maintained by Team TrafficChallan. We don't publish under individual
    bylines because the site's job is to be a consistent, sourced reference — not a personal blog. If you
    spot something wrong, out of date, or missing, see our
    <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a> or just <a href="/contact/">contact us</a> directly.
  </p>
</Base>
